    A cdylib: its one export is wasi:http/handler@0.3.0, whose handle is async-lifted with a callback, and it has no
    wasi:cli/run. Each row is a path; most return their response first and write its body from a spawned future,
    the vendored wasip3 crate's examples/http-proxy.rs shape. What only the guest can see, a reader gone or a
    handler entered, it writes to stderr as one line beginning with the row's name.

THE ORACLE: wasmtime 49.0.3 (f96eabaa1 2026-10-14), .oracle/wasmtime-49.0.3/wasmtime, never the wasmtime on PATH.
build.sh writes its reading to ../p3serve.reading through p3serve_test.go's driver (-update-p3serve): `wasmtime serve
--addr 127.0.0.1:0 --no-logging-prefix --max-instance-reuse-count 1 p3serve.wasm`, its address read from the line it
prints, every row a raw HTTP/1.1 exchange on 127.0.0.1. 10 of 10 drives, each against a fresh server, gave the same
lines, which the driver checks before it writes the reading. --max-instance-reuse-count 1 is the oracle setting: with
wasmtime's default reuse, a burst of 32 concurrent requests failed 4 of 32 (/hello) and 15 of 32 (/big), each a panic
in wit-bindgen's start_task (`assertion failed: task_state::get().is_null()`), and with
--max-instance-concurrent-reuse-count 1 it failed 10 and 16 of 32 the same way (ADR 0137).
